he is 95 years old a few days before the  70th anniversary commemorations Joseph  Kosinski spoke with journalists about  his years in captivity he was number 120  one of more than 700 young poles that  made up the first shipment of slave  laborers to Auschwitz camp commander Col  fridge welcomed them personally I've  never forgotten his words I was standing  in the front row and this is what he  told us you have no idea where you've  ended up this is no sanatorium it's a  German concentration camp your life  expectancy here is three months at the  most priests and Jews will be dead  within six weeks if one can speak of  good fortune at auschwitz patch in ski  seemed to have it he was put to work as  a barber for SS officers one day he was  invited to the senior commanders Villa  to cut his hair I was afraid trembling I  couldn't see straight  his wife escorted me to his bathroom on  the first floor then who doll first  entered he never said a word how you  simply took a seat and I began I'd seen  him before in the barbershop and  observed how my predecessor cut his hair  they did the same patch in ski became  hearses personal barber and so survived  five years of Auschwitz he was among the  first prisoners there and saw the last  train arrive in January 1945 one week  before the camp was liberated he and  hundreds of others were shipped to  another camp patch in ski shunned  Auschwitz for decades after the war his  return this time was still difficult  it stayed with me all these years I  still feel it when I go there I can see  everything so vividly  my friends mothers with children in  their arms all going to their deaths how  could it happen 70 years later Joseph  Kosinski is still seeking the answer to  that question he isn't attending the  70th anniversary commemorations he fears  the memories would be unbearable
 

